The period from 1558 to 1606 covers the colony's development from the end of the [[Second Mahik War]] to the arrival of the Baiduist settlers. It was a period of economic expansion without territorial expansion: the colony's frontier was unchanged, its population multiplied several times over, and its plantation economy grew until the labour on which it depended could no longer be supplied by the means that had built it.

Three developments define the period. The colony's trade ceased to be confined to [[Zong]] and was extended to the other states of [[Fosia]] and, after 1564, to the [[Illypnia|Illypnian]] world. The labour system that had supplied the plantations broke down, and was replaced by a traffic in enslaved people conducted outside the law. And the colony's assembly, a forum of landowners with no power but that of advice, acquired the right to nominate the governor.

Censuses were taken every five years throughout the period, and the returns for 1560 to 1605 are the principal evidence for its economic and social history.

== Censuses ==

The colonial administration continued the practice established in 1540 of enumerating the population every five years, and returns survive for 1560, 1565, 1570, 1575, 1580, 1585, 1590, 1595, 1600 and 1605. The classes of the earlier returns were retained, with one addition: free Zong, indentured Zong, native slaves, free natives and, from 1575, Fosian slaves. The last of these records the arrival of the [[Secret Trade]], and no people from Fosia appear in the returns before that date.

