The free Zong population increased more than fivefold over the half-century, its growth falling when emigration from the mainland was interrupted and rising again when it resumed. The indentured population rose until 1570 and fell thereafter, the decline coinciding with the [[Great Peasant Revolt (Zong)|Great Peasant Revolt]] on the mainland, which disrupted emigration to the colonies. The free native population declined in every return.

The two enslaved classes moved in opposite directions. The native slave population fell in every return, from 8,604 in 1555 to 4,517 in 1605, no new captives being taken in the period and the condition of the enslaved not descending to their children. The Fosian slave population, first recorded in 1575, rose from 2,936 to 40,937 by the end of the period. The whole of the increase in the colony's enslaved population after 1572 is attributable to the trade founded by [[Ong Sam]].

== Plantation economy ==

The colony's exports in the period were plantation crops, timber and fish, and they entered a market that had become saturated. The growth of the plantation economies in [[Sinchew Colony|Sinchew]] and [[Louyang Colony|Louyang]] produced a quantity of colonial goods — spices, sugar, tobacco, cotton, tropical hardwoods and dyes — that exceeded what the Zong market could absorb. The [[Surplus crisis (Zong)|surplus crisis]] that followed ruined domestic producers on the mainland while the colonies that caused it continued to prosper.

The resolution was sought in new markets. Until the middle of the century the colony's trade had been confined by law to Zong, and its cargo had been required to pass through [[Anping]]; from the 1560s the administration at Tamsui pressed for the restriction to be lifted. It was lifted by edict in 1577, during the Great Peasant Revolt, when the mainland was least able to police colonial commerce and most dependent on its revenue.

The edict permitted the colony to trade directly with the other states of Fosia and with the Illypnian powers. Trade with the [[Hachinawa|Hachina]] was exempt from the edict and unaffected by it, having been conducted since the [[Treaty of Anping]] of 1538 under its own terms, principally the exchange of arms for gold.

The period is regarded as the one in which Sinchew became an important point of contact between the Illypnian and Fosian worlds. The [[Great Disappointment]] of 1564, in which [[Volisanians|Volisanian]] explorers reached the Fosian colonies, brought the Illypnian powers into the region, and the colony's harbour and its position on the western coast made it a place at which the commerce and the diplomacy of the two spheres met.

== Labour ==

The plantation economy required labour, and the means by which the colony had formerly obtained it ceased to be sufficient in this period.

Indentured settlers arrived from Zong on contracts of ten years and in increasing numbers until 1570. Most returned to Zong when their terms expired. The climate of the colony was not hospitable to settlers from the eastern coast, and there was little work for a freed labourer to take, so that the greater part of those whose contracts ended left the colony rather than remain in it. Those who stayed became landowners themselves, and as landowners they wanted labour of their own rather than to supply it.

The indenture system broke in the 1570s. The [[Great Peasant Revolt (Zong)|Great Peasant Revolt]] of 1572 to 1585 disrupted emigration from the mainland, and with it the supply of indentured settlers, which fell by more than a third between 1570 and 1580.

The colony's other source of labour was the enslavement of the indigenous peoples of the region. That population was declining throughout the period, under the pressure of war, epidemic disease and the practice of enslavement itself. It had also a further limitation: slavery among the region's peoples, as the colony practised it, was not of a kind that descended from parent to child, and the children of the enslaved were born free. The enslaved population therefore could not replace itself, and required a constant supply of newly captured people merely to remain constant in size.

== Secret Trade ==

{{Main|Secret Trade}}

The traffic that supplied the deficiency was founded in 1572 by [[Ong Sam]] (王三, 1545–1620), a Zong merchant who had settled in Sinchew in the 1560s. Visiting the Burawi colony of [[Lacashe]] in 1572, he observed the plantation system and the wealth that enslaved labour produced, and returned determined to establish the like in Sinchew.

The constraint was the [[Zong Edict of 1500]], which prohibited slavery in all territories of the kingdom. Ong Sam's answer was to procure labour without purchasing it: raiders, drawn from the pirates of the [[Shendan Ocean]] and mercenaries recruited from [[Yanbaru]] and [[Nakamizu]], were employed to seize people from the coastal settlements of [[Dagit]] and the [[Balausia Peninsula]] and to deliver them to intermediaries in Sinchew, who sold them to planters.

The enslaved people brought by this traffic were held as property, and their condition descended to their children. The trade accordingly provided what the enslavement of the region's peoples could not: a labouring population capable of reproducing itself. It expanded through the remainder of the period and beyond, and the censuses record its effect in the growth of the enslaved population from 13,220 in 1570 to 43,220 in 1605.

== Government ==

The General Assembly of Sinchew, established in 1549 as a forum of the colony's landowners with no power beyond that of advising the governor, increased its influence over successive administrations in this period. The governors continued to be appointed from Zong, but the forum's views on taxation, land and trade were increasingly sought before appointments were made and before policy was settled.

A change of substance followed from an accident. During the [[Great Peasant Revolt (Zong)|Great Peasant Revolt]], the governor of the colony died in office with no successor designated and the vice-governor absent from the colony. The forum, rather than leave the administration vacant for the months a decision from Zong would require, appointed a governor of its own, [[Ng Hui-seng]] (黃惠生), a planter of [[Tamsui]], to hold the office until a governor should be appointed by the crown.

No appointment came. Zong was occupied with the revolt and with the disorders that followed it, and the administration of the colony passed into the hands of a man holding office by the forum's choice alone. The arrangement was regularised after the restoration, and the practice that emerged from it — that the governor was nominated by the forum for appointment by the crown — became the established form. The office remained the crown's to confer, but the name submitted for it was the colony's.

== Frontier ==

The colony did not extend its territory in this period. Its frontier with the [[Hachinawa|Hachina]] state was unchanged, and the period was one of peace and regular trade along it.

The indigenous polities to the south and east consolidated during the same years. In 1565 the [[Nahola]] states joined the Hachina empire, and in 1568 [[Uwisa]] did the same, in a period in which the native states of the region were being gathered under a single polity. The colony's oldest ally was therefore its largest neighbour by the end of the 1560s, and the two powers maintained a border that neither tested.

The Zong administration nevertheless drew plans for the invasion of Hachina territory during this period. None was executed. The colony's interest lay in the trade that peace permitted, and the value of that trade, which included the arms the Hachina required and the gold with which they paid for them, was greater than the land beyond the frontier was thought to be worth.

== Baiduist influx ==

The period closed with the [[Baiduist Expulsion from Zong]] and the transportation of more than a million Zong Baiduists to the colony from 1606. The settlement of that population transformed Sinchew, and the labour it supplied answered a deficiency that the indenture system, the enslavement of the region's peoples and the Secret Trade had each failed to remedy.

